# Every path and port the container's parts have to agree on, in one file.
#
# Before this, the same literals were spelled out in each script, in
# setup/app.py and in router.psgi -- three languages, each with its own copy
# of "/data/publicinbox/config" and of which port the dashboard sits on. They
# only stayed in step by hand, and a value changed in one place and missed in
# another fails at runtime, not at build time. (They had already drifted: the
# dashboard read SETUP_HOST/SETUP_PORT for exactly what router.psgi called
# DASHBOARD_HOST/DASHBOARD_PORT.)
#
# entrypoint.sh sources this with `set -a' and everything it starts inherits
# the result, so the shell scripts, the Python dashboard and the Perl router
# all read the same values out of the environment. Each loop script sources
# it again so it still works when run on its own.
#
# Every assignment is `:=', so anything already set in the environment wins:
# `podman run -e DASHBOARD_PORT=...' still overrides, and sourcing this twice
# is harmless. Order matters -- later entries build on earlier ones.

# Volume root. Everything that has to survive a container replacement hangs
# off this.
: "${DATA_DIR:=/data}"

# korgalore + liblore, resynced from git on every start by sync-venv.sh
# rather than baked into the image.
: "${VENV_DIR:=$DATA_DIR/venv}"
: "${KGL:=$VENV_DIR/bin/kgl}"

# XDG dirs are set as ENV in the Containerfile so an interactive `podman
# exec ... kgl' sees them too; repeated here so a standalone run of any of
# these scripts outside the container still lands on the volume.
: "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:=$DATA_DIR/xdg/config}"
: "${XDG_DATA_HOME:=$DATA_DIR/xdg/data}"
: "${KORGALORE_CONF_PATH:=$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/korgalore/korgalore.toml}"
: "${LEI_ARCHIVES_PATH:=$XDG_DATA_HOME/korgalore/lei}"
# korgalore loads conf.d from beside its own config, and `kgl
# track-subsystem' writes one file per subsystem there -- which makes it
# the list of what this container tracks, and where each subsystem's mail
# was delivered. lei's saved searches say which query filled each one.
: "${KORGALORE_CONFD_PATH:=$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/korgalore/conf.d}"
: "${LEI_SAVED_SEARCHES_PATH:=$XDG_DATA_HOME/lei/saved-searches}"

# The one externally-published port (README, "Ports"): 11043 is leetspeak
# for LLORE, "local lore". ROUTER_PUBLIC_BASE is what public-inbox embeds in
# self-referential links, so it has to be the address a browser reaches --
# set it to the real site name when fronting this with a proxy.
: "${ROUTER_PORT:=11043}"
: "${ROUTER_PUBLIC_BASE:=http://127.0.0.1:$ROUTER_PORT}"
: "${ROUTER_SCRIPT:=/usr/local/lib/maintainer-setup/router.psgi}"

# How many worker processes public-inbox-netd forks for the router. Not a
# throughput knob -- it is isolation. The router reverse-proxies to the
# dashboard and to the MCP server, and proxy_to() blocks the process it
# runs in until the upstream answers; an MCP tool call that spends four
# seconds in `lei' therefore spends four seconds during which that process
# serves nobody. With -W0 that process is the only one there is, so a
# single slow proxied request also stops cgit, git clones, and the IMAP
# and NNTP listeners, which live in the same daemon. Workers give the
# blocking call somewhere to block that is not everyone else's event
# loop. Nothing here keeps per-process state that has to be shared:
# load_pi_config() re-reads on mtime in whichever worker takes the
# request, so each one refreshes itself.
#
# Two was too close to the edge. A pair of /mcp requests that never
# finished took both of them and the archive stopped answering entirely --
# a blocked worker is also outside the event loop, so it ignores the
# graceful SIGTERM and needs SIGKILL. MCP_PROXY_DEADLINE below is the
# actual fix; the extra workers are headroom so that one stuck request is
# a quarter of the capacity rather than half of it.
: "${WEB_WORKERS:=4}"

# Wall-clock bound, in seconds, on one proxied /mcp request. Separate from
# the router's read timeout, which HTTP::Tiny applies per read and which a
# backend that emits anything at all resets indefinitely -- two /mcp
# requests once outlived it entirely and, because the proxy blocks the
# worker it runs in, stopped the archive, cgit, git clones and the mail
# listeners along with themselves. Raise it if a tool call legitimately
# needs longer; every MCP tool here answers in seconds.
: "${MCP_PROXY_DEADLINE:=120}"

# The dashboard's own listener, internal only -- router.psgi proxies to it
# and nothing else should reach it. 11044 is simply the public port plus
# one; two processes can't accept() on one port, which is the whole reason
# for the split.
: "${DASHBOARD_HOST:=127.0.0.1}"
: "${DASHBOARD_PORT:=11044}"

# The MCP server's own listener, internal only, on the same terms as the
# dashboard: router.psgi proxies /mcp to it and nothing else should reach
# it. 11045 continues the run -- 11043 published, 11044 dashboard.
: "${MCP_HOST:=127.0.0.1}"
: "${MCP_PORT:=11045}"

# public-inbox's config and the cross-inbox Xapian index behind /lore/all/.
# The stamp records when kgl-pull-loop.sh last indexed successfully.
: "${PI_CONFIG:=$DATA_DIR/publicinbox/config}"
: "${PUBLICINBOX_EXTINDEX_PATH:=$DATA_DIR/publicinbox/extindex}"
: "${EXTINDEX_STAMP_PATH:=$DATA_DIR/publicinbox/.extindex-stamp}"

# grokmirror's local mirror: the toplevel git clones live under, its config,
# and the manifest of what it has.
: "${GROKMIRROR_DIR:=$DATA_DIR/grokmirror}"
: "${GROKMIRROR_TOPLEVEL:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/repos}"
: "${GROKMIRROR_CONF_PATH:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/grokmirror.conf}"
: "${GROKMIRROR_MANIFEST_PATH:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/manifest.js.gz}"

# grok-pull-loop.sh owns the grok-pull process and records it in the pid
# file; the dashboard reads both of these to show a mirror's progress,
# since it never runs grok-pull itself (see grok-pull-loop.sh's header for
# why). The log is also what [core] log points at in the grokmirror.conf
# the dashboard generates.
: "${GROKMIRROR_PID_PATH:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/grok-pull.pid}"
: "${GROKMIRROR_LOG_PATH:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/grok-pull.log}"

# Shared-object repos, deliberately a sibling of the toplevel rather than
# grokmirror's own default of $GROKMIRROR_TOPLEVEL/objstore. They carry no
# branches or history of their own -- just a pile of objects that several
# real repos reach through alternates -- so there is nothing in them worth
# browsing, and anything that walks the toplevel looking for repos (cgit's
# scan-path, router.psgi's clone route) lists them as if there were. Keeping
# them out of the tree in the first place is cheaper and more reliable than
# teaching each of those walkers to skip them.
: "${GROKMIRROR_OBJSTORE:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/objstore}"

# Where `preload' (preload-objstore.sh) leaves an object store it built out
# of a clone the maintainer already had, laid out by upstream path the way
# the toplevel is. The dashboard moves it into GROKMIRROR_OBJSTORE once that
# repo is picked, which is a rename only because both are on this volume --
# keep the two side by side.
: "${GROKMIRROR_PRELOAD_DIR:=$GROKMIRROR_DIR/preload}"

# The site grokmirror mirrors from. The dashboard fetches its manifest and
# points grok-pull at it; preload-objstore.sh asks it which tags are real.
: "${GROKMIRROR_SITE:=https://git.kernel.org}"

# Optional protocol listeners, all off until the maintainer turns them on
# from the dashboard, which writes its choice to DAEMONS_PATH. The same
# public-inbox-netd that serves the web port also speaks IMAP and NNTP
# (PublicInbox::IMAP, PublicInbox::NNTP, reached through Daemon.pm's
# load_mod), so turning one on adds a listener to that one process rather
# than starting another -- see serve-web.sh. Ports follow the container's
# 11xxx convention, last three digits echoing the well-known port.
#
# Note that enabling one only binds it *inside* the container. Publishing
# it is podman's job and has to be asked for when the container is created:
# `-p 127.0.0.1:11143:11143'.
: "${DAEMONS_PATH:=$DATA_DIR/enabled-daemons.json}"
# Only ever printed, never bound: the address the dashboard tells the
# maintainer to publish an optional listener on. It has to match however
# the router port was published, and nothing can check that for us --
# a container cannot see its own port mappings, and podman will not take
# a hostname in --publish. Set it beside PublishPort when that is not
# loopback.
: "${PUBLISH_ADDRESS:=127.0.0.1}"
: "${IMAP_PORT:=11143}"
: "${NNTP_PORT:=11119}"

# How often each background loop comes around, in seconds.
: "${VENV_SYNC_INTERVAL:=86400}"
: "${KGL_PULL_INTERVAL:=600}"
: "${GROKMIRROR_FSCK_INTERVAL:=86400}"
